---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import type { ArticleMeta } from '../../types/article';
import { collectPosts } from '../../lib/posts';

export const meta: ArticleMeta = {
  title: 'PS5 vs Xbox Series X: Which Console Should You Buy?',
  description:
    'PS5 vs Xbox Series X compared on specs, games, storage, and price. A clear verdict for each kind of player, plus who should pick neither.',
  category: 'console',
  type: 'comparison',
  keyword: 'ps5 vs xbox series x',
  date: '2026-09-19',
  updated: '2026-09-19',
  image: '/images/placeholder.svg',
  featured: false,
  affiliateUrl: 'https://www.amazon.com/s?k=playstation+5+console',
  author: 'Lucas Hart',
  authorBio:
    'Lucas Hart has spent five years testing PlayStation hardware, from hall effect controllers to SSD storage upgrades, and writes hands-on buying guides for Gearky.',
};

const related = collectPosts(import.meta.glob('./*.astro', { eager: true }))
  .filter((p) => p.title !== meta.title)
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<ArticleLayout
  meta={meta}
  related={related}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Which is more powerful, the PS5 or Xbox Series X?',
      a: 'On paper the Xbox Series X has a higher GPU peak at 12 teraflops versus 10.28 on the PS5. In practice the faster PS5 SSD closes the gap, and real-world games look nearly identical on both.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does the PS5 or Xbox Series X have more storage?',
      a: 'The Series X ships with 1TB, the PS5 with 825GB. But the PS5 lets you add any standard M.2 SSD, while the Series X uses a pricier proprietary expansion card.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Which console has better exclusive games?',
      a: 'The PS5 has a larger library of single-player exclusives like God of War Ragnarok, Spider-Man 2, and Astro Bot. The Xbox strength is Game Pass value rather than exclusives.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is Game Pass better than PS Plus?',
      a: 'For most people, yes. Game Pass Ultimate includes day-one first-party games for about $17 a month, which is hard to beat on value even though PS Plus offers a similar structure.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The ps5 vs xbox series x decision comes down to two things that barely show
    up in a spec sheet: which games you want and which subscription you will
    actually pay for. The raw hardware is close enough that you could not tell
    them apart in a blind test. I have owned both since launch, and here is the
    honest comparison, including the use cases where neither console is the
    right buy.
  </p>

  <h2>Specs: the differences that do not matter</h2>

  <p>
    Both consoles run eight-core AMD Zen 2 CPUs and 16GB of GDDR6 memory. The
    Xbox Series X has a larger GPU on paper, 12 teraflops against the PS5's
    10.28, but the PS5 counters with a much faster SSD, 5.5 GB/s raw against
    2.4 GB/s. The result is that games on both machines hit the same 4K at 60
    frames per second targets, and differences in visual quality are measured
    in pixels you need a zoom tool to see. Chasing the spec sheet is a dead end.
  </p>

  <table class="spec-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Spec</th>
        <th>PS5</th>
        <th>Xbox Series X</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>CPU</td>
        <td>8-core Zen 2, 3.5 GHz</td>
        <td>8-core Zen 2, 3.8 GHz</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>GPU</td>
        <td>10.28 TF, 36 CUs</td>
        <td>12 TF, 52 CUs</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Storage</td>
        <td>825GB custom SSD</td>
        <td>1TB custom NVMe</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Expansion</td>
        <td>Standard M.2 slot</td>
        <td>Proprietary card</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Disc version price</td>
        <td>$499</td>
        <td>$499</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Digital version price</td>
        <td>$449</td>
        <td>$449</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Games: the real decision</h2>

  <p>
    This is where the two consoles split. Sony's first-party lineup leans hard
    into single-player blockbusters. God of War Ragnarok, Marvel's Spider-Man
    2, Astro Bot, and Returnal are all PS5 exclusives, and they are the main
    reason people buy the console. If you care about those franchises, the
    choice is already made.
  </p>

  <p>
    Xbox's answer is Game Pass, not exclusives. The subscription includes
    first-party games on day one, from Forza to Halo to the next Bethesda
    release, for about $17 a month. The Series X has fewer must-play exclusives
    than the PS5, but the value of a hundred-plus game library is hard to
    argue with if you play a lot of different games rather than one or two
    big releases a year.
  </p>

  <h2>Storage and upgrades</h2>

  <p>
    The PS5 wins this category outright. When the 825GB fills up, you open the
    side panel and slot in any standard M.2 Gen4 SSD. A 2TB upgrade costs
    around $160 and takes ten minutes. The Series X makes you buy a proprietary
    expansion card that has historically cost far more per gigabyte. That is a
    real, ongoing cost if you keep a large library installed.
  </p>

  <h2>The controller</h2>

  <p>
    The DualSense is the more interesting pad. Its haptic feedback and adaptive
    triggers genuinely change how supported games feel, and once you have
    played Returnal with the triggers pushing back, going back to a static pad
    feels flat. The Xbox controller is excellent but conventional. If you care
    about controller innovation, the PS5 is ahead, even though the Xbox pad is
    slightly more comfortable for long sessions in my hands.
  </p>

  <h2>PS5 vs Xbox Series X: the verdict by player type</h2>

  <p>
    Buy the PS5 if you want Sony's single-player exclusives, a better
    controller, and cheaper storage upgrades. Buy the Xbox Series X if you want
    the best subscription value in gaming and do not care which console your
    friends are on. If you mostly play free-to-play multiplayer like Fortnite
    or Apex, the choice barely matters; pick the one your friends use, because
    that is the whole game.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=playstation+5+console" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the PS5
    </a>
    <span class="sep">·</span>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=xbox+series+x" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the Xbox Series X
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Who should buy neither</h2>

  <p>
    Skip both if you already have a gaming PC. Xbox first-party games land on
    PC through Game Pass anyway, and Sony has been bringing its exclusives to
    PC a year or two after launch. A capable PC plus a controller gives you
    most of what both consoles offer, with better graphics and no console tax.
    If you play exclusively on one platform already, a second $500 box is
    rarely worth it.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .spec-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .spec-table th,
  .spec-table td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .spec-table th {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
  .buy-link {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .sep {
    margin: 0 var(--space-2);
    color: var(--color-text-faint);
  }
</style>
